<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1924–1988</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Zia-ul-Haq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq was a Pakistani army general who overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a> in 1977 and ruled as military ruler and president until his death. He had Bhutto hanged in 1979, pursued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> of law and education, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> the main base for the Afghan mujahideen fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. He died in an unexplained aircraft crash in 1988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-zia-ul-haq.jpg" alt="Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pakistan Army · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Muhammad_Zia-Ul-Haq.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
